module lib/hf/hermite
  The two tables every McMurchie-Davidson integral is built from.

  McMurchie-Davidson is a way to integrate products of Gaussians. The product
  of two Gaussians on centers A and B, exponents $a$ and $b$, is one Gaussian
  on a point P between them, with exponent $p = a + b$, times a polynomial.
  McMurchie and Davidson write that polynomial as a sum of Hermite Gaussians:
  derivatives of the plain Gaussian $e^{-p (x - P_x)^2}$ with respect to
  $P_x$. The coefficients of that sum, one axis at a time, are the E table,
  $E(i, j, t)$ for powers $i$ on A, $j$ on B and derivative order $t$.

  A derivative with respect to P passes through an integral over the
  electron's position, so each integral becomes the E coefficients times
  derivatives of one function of P. For the $1/r$ integrals (nuclear
  attraction, electron repulsion) that function is the Boys function $F_0$,
  and its derivatives are the R table, $R(t, u, v)$, one derivative order
  per axis. This module fills both tables from the bottom up, so each entry
  is computed once, into flat buffers a caller reuses for every primitive
  pair or quartet.

herm_nh  @30c06c91
  why   The length of a Hermite index list and the count of R entries an
        integral of total order L uses.
  L     the total derivative order, $t + u + v \le L$

herm_tuv  @16cccd0c
  why   The integral codes loop over Hermite functions by one index; this is
        the map from that index to $(t, u, v)$.
  L     the total order
  H     the list being filled, `[nh, 3]`
  h     the next row of H

herm_esize  @5901b9db
  why   Callers size one E buffer per axis from it before a loop over
        primitive pairs.
  la    the highest power on center A
  lb    the highest power on center B

herm_efill  @e4f648f1
  why   The E coefficients of a primitive pair, computed once per pair and
        axis, and read by every integral over that pair. Filling upward from
        $E(0, 0, 0)$ computes each entry once; recursing downward for each
        wanted entry, as lib/gto_md did, computes the same entries many times
        over.
  E      the buffer written, from `e0` on
  e0     where this table starts in `E`
  la     the highest power on A
  lb     the highest power on B
  a      A's exponent
  b      B's exponent
  X      $X_{AB} = A - B$ on this axis
  nt     the number of $t$ values per $(i, j)$, `la + lb + 1`
  ld     the number of $j$ values, `lb + 1`
  p      $p = a + b$, the exponent of the product
  o2p    $\frac{1}{2p}$
  xpa    $X_{PA}$  the product center minus A, $-b X_{AB} / p$
  xpb    $X_{PB}$  the product center minus B, $a X_{AB} / p$
  src    where the row being raised from starts
  dst    where the row being written starts
  v      the entry being computed

  $$ E_{i+1,j}^{t} = \frac{1}{2p} E_{ij}^{t-1} + X_{PA} E_{ij}^{t} + (t+1) E_{ij}^{t+1} $$  [test "E reproduces the Gaussian product pointwise for i, j <= 3"]
  = E#2  the start: $E(0, 0, 0) = e^{-\mu X_{AB}^2}$ with $\mu = ab/p$, this axis's share of the product's prefactor
  = E#3  raise $i$ with $j = 0$, by the recursion above
  = E#4  then raise $j$ for every $i$, the same recursion with $X_{PB}$

herm_etab  @3e9d6a50
  why   The shape the frozen interface and the tests
        read, `E[i, j, t]`, copied out of the flat table.
  la    the highest power on A
  lb    the highest power on B
  a     A's exponent
  b     B's exponent
  XAB   A minus B on this axis
  F     the flat table
  nt    the number of $t$ values
  E     the result, `[la+1, lb+1, la+lb+1]`

herm_rfill  @34a9076b
  why   The R table of one Coulomb integral: every derivative of
        $F_0(\alpha |P - C|^2)$ an integral of total order L needs, from one
        Boys evaluation at the top order.
  S      the buffer: `2 (L+1)^3` entries, two cubes that hold alternate levels
  L      the total order
  alpha  $\alpha$  the exponent in the Boys argument: $p$ for a nucleus,
         $pq/(p+q)$ for a pair of pairs
  X      $X$  the $x$ component of $P - C$; Y and Z likewise
  F      $F_0 \ldots F_L$ at $\alpha (X^2 + Y^2 + Z^2)$
  pref   a factor applied to every entry, so the caller need not scale after
  D      the side of one cube, `L + 1`
  D2     $D^2$
  D3     $D^3$, the size of one cube
  m2a    $-2\alpha$
  pw     $\mathrm{pref}\,(-2\alpha)^n$ for the current level
  cur    the start of the cube being written
  prv    the start of the cube read, the level above
  n      the level being written; level `0` is the answer
  m      the highest total order level `n` needs

  $$ R_{n}(t+1, u, v) = t R_{n+1}(t-1, u, v) + X R_{n+1}(t, u, v) $$  [test "R(2, 0, 0) and R(1, 1, 0) match the chain rule on F_0"]
  = S#2    the top level holds one entry, $(-2\alpha)^L F_L$
  = cur#2  each level writes the cube the level above did not
  = S#3    the level's base entry, $(-2\alpha)^n F_n$
  = S#4    entries with $t = 1$: only the $X$ term
  = S#5    entries with $t \ge 2$: the recursion along $x$
  = S#6    $t = 0$, $u = 1$: the $Y$ term alone
  = S#7    $t = 0$, $u \ge 2$: the recursion along $y$
  = S#8    $t = u = 0$, $v = 1$
  = S#9    $t = u = 0$, $v \ge 2$: the recursion along $z$

herm_rfill_box  @ac1715c4
  why   An integral over one component of each shell needs R only for
        $t \le T_x$, $u \le T_y$, $v \le T_z$; building only that box saves
        most of the cube for components that point along one axis
        [test "the box R equals the full cube wherever both are defined"].
  S      the buffer: two boxes from `s0` on
  s0     where the boxes start
  Tx     the highest $t$ needed; Ty and Tz likewise for $u$ and $v$
  alpha  $\alpha$  the exponent in the Boys argument
  X      the $x$ component of $P - C$; Y and Z likewise
  F      $F_0 \ldots F_L$
  pref   a factor applied to every entry
  L      $T_x + T_y + T_z$, the total order
  DV     the stride of $u$ in a box
  DU     the stride of $t$
  C      the size of one box
  m2a    $-2\alpha$
  n      the level being written
  cur    the start of the box being written
  prv    the start of the box read
  pw     $\mathrm{pref}\,(-2\alpha)^n$
  o      the offset of $(t, u, v)$ in a box
  w      the entry being computed

  = cur  levels alternate between the two boxes by the parity of $n$
  = w#1  $t \ge 1$: the recursion along $x$
  = w#3  $t = 0$, $u \ge 1$: along $y$
  = w#5  $t = u = 0$, $v \ge 1$: along $z$

herm_rtab  @2a6abd1b
  why   The frozen interface's form `R[t, u, v]`, for the tests and the
        one-electron code, copied out of the flat buffer.
  L     the total order
  p     the exponent
  PC    $P - C$, `[3]`
  F     the Boys values at $p |PC|^2$
  D     the side of the cube
  S     the flat buffer herm_rfill writes
  R     the result, `[L+1, L+1, L+1]`

herm_grid  @455defa7
  why   herm_boys_fill runs once per primitive quartet and must not call
        boys_switch there, since that builds its list each time; the grid
        carries the switches with it.
  nmax  the highest order the grid serves
  xmax  the largest argument expected
  G     the grid of boys_grid_new, with the switches in its header row

herm_boys_fill  @26420582
  why   The Boys values of the innermost loop of every $1/r$ integral, written
        into the caller's buffer. It gives the same numbers as boys_grid_eval
        [test "the in-place Boys fill equals boys_values to 1e-14 across the grid, the switch and the tail"],
        without allocating an array per call.
  G     the grid from herm_grid
  nmax  the highest order wanted
  x     the argument
  F     filled with $F_0 \ldots F_{n_{\max}}$
  ex    $e^{-x}$
  xs    the switch for order `nmax`
  i     the index of the nearest grid node
  r     that node's row in `G`
  top   $F_{n_{\max}}(x)$
  md    $x_0 - x$, the node minus the argument
  c     the node's value of $F_{n_{\max}}$

  = xs#2   the switch, read from the grid's header when the grid covers this order
  = F#1    above the switch: $F_0 = \frac12\sqrt{\pi/x}$, then upward
  = top#2  outside the grid: the series
  = top#3  the Taylor step from the nearest node, in Horner form [test "the Taylor step in herm_boys_fill has the grid's 8 extra orders"]
  = F#4    the lower orders by the downward step

herm_lambda  @4e9b214f
  why   The tests check E against the Hermite Gaussians it expands into, and
        this computes them by a different route.
  t     the derivative order
  p     the exponent
  xp    $x - P$
  s     $\sqrt{p}$
  z     $\sqrt{p}\,(x - P)$, the argument of the Hermite polynomial
  h     $H_t(z)$; h0 and h1 are the two orders below it

herm_overlap_1d  @a7abf623
  why   A second route to the 1-D overlap, from the binomial expansion and the
        Gaussian moments, so the test of $E(i, j, 0)$ does not check E against
        itself.
  i     the power on A
  j     the power on B
  a     A's exponent
  b     B's exponent
  X     A minus B
  p     $a + b$
  xpa   $X_{PA}$
  xpb   $X_{PB}$
  tot   the sum over the binomial terms
  cr    the binomial coefficient $\binom{i}{r}$
  cs    the binomial coefficient $\binom{j}{s}$
  m     the power of $(x - P)$ in a term
  mom   the Gaussian moment $\int (x - P)^m e^{-p (x - P)^2} dx$
